window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["fastapi"]=[{"id":"fastapi-f0001","card":"q-fastapi-001-1-fastapi-overview-and-setup","sec":"fastapi-s002","q":"What does FastAPI give you, and what does it deliberately not do itself?","a":"<p>FastAPI is a web framework for building APIs. It gives you routing, request parsing, validation (through Pydantic), serialization and automatic OpenAPI documentation at <code>/docs</code>. It does not implement an HTTP server. It speaks ASGI, and an ASGI server such as Uvicorn accepts the connections and hands requests to it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0002","card":"q-fastapi-001-1-fastapi-overview-and-setup","sec":"fastapi-s002","q":"What is ASGI, and why does FastAPI use it instead of WSGI?","a":"<p>ASGI is the asynchronous successor of WSGI, the Python interface between web servers and applications. WSGI is synchronous, one request per worker call. ASGI supports <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>, long-lived connections (WebSockets, streaming) and concurrent I/O on one event loop. FastAPI is built on Starlette, an ASGI framework, so it needs an ASGI server like Uvicorn or Hypercorn.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0003","card":"q-fastapi-001-1-fastapi-overview-and-setup","sec":"fastapi-s002","q":"Which libraries does FastAPI rely on for the web layer and for validation?","a":"<p>Starlette provides the web layer (routing, requests, responses, middleware, ASGI plumbing). Pydantic provides data validation and serialization from Python type hints. FastAPI adds dependency injection and OpenAPI schema building on top of them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0004","card":"q-fastapi-001-1-fastapi-overview-and-setup","sec":"fastapi-s002","q":"Why does <code>pip install \"fastapi[standard]\"</code> differ from <code>pip install fastapi</code>, and when do you want the extra?","a":"<p>The <code>standard</code> extra also installs commonly needed pieces: Uvicorn, the <code>fastapi</code> command-line tool, <code>python-multipart</code> (needed for form data) and <code>httpx</code> (used by the test client). The bare package is just the framework. Use <code>standard</code> for normal development. Use the bare package plus hand-picked dependencies for slim production images.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0005","card":"q-fastapi-001-1-fastapi-overview-and-setup","sec":"fastapi-s002","q":"In FastAPI, what makes it \"fast\"?","a":"<p>Two things. Development speed: type hints give you parsing, validation and docs, so a working endpoint takes few lines. Runtime speed: it runs on an async ASGI stack (Starlette and Uvicorn) and Pydantic v2 validates in Rust, so it handles many concurrent I/O-bound requests efficiently. It is not faster for CPU-bound work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0006","card":"q-fastapi-001-1-fastapi-overview-and-setup","sec":"fastapi-s002","q":"Why pin a version range such as <code>fastapi[standard] ~= 0.112.0</code> in <code>requirements.txt</code>?","a":"<p>The compatible-release operator <code>~= 0.112.0</code> allows patch updates (0.112.x) but not 0.113. FastAPI is still on a 0.x version, so minor releases can change behavior. Pinning a range gives reproducible builds while still getting bug fixes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0007","card":"q-fastapi-001-1-fastapi-overview-and-setup","sec":"fastapi-s002","q":"A FastAPI server returns this from a handler: <code>return {'errors': None}</code>. What does the client receive?","a":"<p>A <code>200 OK</code> response with <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> and the body <code>{\"errors\":null}</code>. FastAPI serializes dicts, lists and Pydantic models to JSON automatically, and Python <code>None</code> becomes JSON <code>null</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0008","card":"q-fastapi-002-2-rest-http-and-json-fundamentals","sec":"fastapi-s003","q":"What is REST, and how do CRUD operations map to HTTP verbs?","a":"<p>REST is an architectural style where a server exposes things (collections of things) and clients act on them over HTTP, usually with JSON bodies. Create is <code>POST</code>, read is <code>GET</code>, update is <code>PUT</code> (replace) or <code>PATCH</code> (partial change), delete is <code>DELETE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0009","card":"q-fastapi-002-2-rest-http-and-json-fundamentals","sec":"fastapi-s003","q":"What is the difference between <code>PUT</code> and <code>PATCH</code>, and which one is idempotent?","a":"<p><code>PUT</code> replaces the whole resource with the body you send. <code>PATCH</code> applies a partial change. <code>PUT</code> is idempotent by definition: sending it twice leaves the same state. <code>PATCH</code> may or may not be idempotent, depending on the operation (a \"set field\" patch is, an \"increment\" patch is not). <code>POST</code> is not idempotent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0010","card":"q-fastapi-002-2-rest-http-and-json-fundamentals","sec":"fastapi-s003","q":"Why do clients of a REST API care about idempotency, and which verbs are safe to retry automatically?","a":"<p>After a timeout the client does not know if the request succeeded. Idempotent methods (<code>GET</code>, <code>PUT</code>, <code>DELETE</code>, <code>HEAD</code>) give the same final state when repeated, so a retry is safe. Retrying <code>POST</code> can create duplicates, so it needs an idempotency key or a server-side duplicate check.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0011","card":"q-fastapi-002-2-rest-http-and-json-fundamentals","sec":"fastapi-s003","q":"Why should a developer using FastAPI still understand raw HTTP and JSON?","a":"<p>The framework hides the wire format, but problems show up there: a missing <code>Content-Length</code>, a wrong <code>Content-Type</code>, a proxy that limits body size, a datetime that arrives as a string. Debugging with <code>curl</code> or a packet trace requires knowing what a request and response look like.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0012","card":"q-fastapi-003-http-protocol","sec":"fastapi-s004","q":"What does an HTTP/1.1 request look like on the wire, and which parts does it have?","a":"<p>It is plain text: a request line (verb, path, HTTP version), header lines, an empty line, then an optional body. A response has a status line (<code>HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code>), headers, an empty line and the body. The blank line is what separates headers from body.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0013","card":"q-fastapi-003-http-protocol","sec":"fastapi-s004","q":"In HTTP/1.1, which request header is required, and why?","a":"<p><code>Host</code>. One server IP can host many sites, and <code>Host</code> tells the server which one the client wants (virtual hosting). A request without it is rejected with <code>400</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0014","card":"q-fastapi-003-http-protocol","sec":"fastapi-s004","q":"What does <code>Content-Length</code> count, and what happens if it does not match the body?","a":"<p>It counts the bytes of the body only, not the headers. If it is smaller than the real body, the server reads a truncated body and may treat the rest as the next request. If it is larger, the server waits for more data until it times out. Servers and proxies often reject mismatches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0015","card":"q-fastapi-003-http-protocol","sec":"fastapi-s004","q":"What does <code>Connection: close</code> do in an HTTP/1.1 request?","a":"<p>It tells the server to close the TCP connection after sending the response. HTTP/1.1 keeps connections open by default (keep-alive), so <code>close</code> is used when you want one request per connection, for example in a quick manual test.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0016","card":"q-fastapi-003-http-protocol","sec":"fastapi-s004","q":"What is the difference between <code>400</code>, <code>401</code>, <code>403</code>, <code>404</code> and <code>422</code>, and when would a FastAPI service return each?","a":"<p><code>400</code> is a malformed or invalid request that you reject yourself. <code>401</code> means not authenticated, <code>403</code> means authenticated but not allowed. <code>404</code> means the resource does not exist. <code>422 Unprocessable Entity</code> is what FastAPI returns automatically when a request is well-formed but fails Pydantic validation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0017","card":"q-fastapi-003-http-protocol","sec":"fastapi-s004","q":"What do the status code classes <code>2xx</code>, <code>4xx</code> and <code>5xx</code> tell a client about who should fix a failure?","a":"<p><code>2xx</code> is success. <code>4xx</code> means the client sent something wrong, so retrying the same request will not help. <code>5xx</code> means the server failed, so a retry may succeed later. This is why an unhandled exception that returns <code>500</code> for bad input is a design flaw: it blames the server for a client mistake.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0018","card":"q-fastapi-004-json","sec":"fastapi-s005","q":"How do JSON types map to Python types?","a":"<p>Object is <code>dict</code>, array is <code>list</code>, string is <code>str</code>, number is <code>int</code> or <code>float</code>, true/false is <code>bool</code>, and null is <code>None</code>. JSON has one number type, so Python's <code>Decimal</code> and <code>Fraction</code> have no direct equivalent. <code>tuple</code> becomes an array, <code>set</code> has no JSON form.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0019","card":"q-fastapi-004-json","sec":"fastapi-s005","q":"How do you send a datetime or binary data in JSON, given that JSON has no types for them?","a":"<p>A datetime is sent as a string, usually ISO 8601 such as <code>2024-01-02T13:42:09Z</code>, and the receiver must parse it. Binary data is usually Base64-encoded into a string, which adds about 33% size. For large binary payloads, send the raw bytes with a binary media type instead of JSON.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0020","card":"q-fastapi-004-json","sec":"fastapi-s005","q":"Why can large integers or money values be risky in JSON?","a":"<p>JSON numbers have no defined precision. A client such as JavaScript parses them as 64-bit floats, so integers larger than 2^53 lose precision, and floats cannot represent decimal money exactly. Send large IDs as strings and money as integer cents (or a decimal string).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0021","card":"q-fastapi-004-json","sec":"fastapi-s005","q":"What does FastAPI do for you on JSON encoding and decoding?","a":"<p>For requests, it parses the JSON body and validates it into the types you declared. For responses, it converts dicts, lists, datetimes, enums, UUIDs and Pydantic models into JSON-compatible values. You rarely call <code>json.dumps</code> yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0022","card":"q-fastapi-005-3-handlers-running-the-server-sync-vs-as","sec":"fastapi-s006","q":"What is a handler in FastAPI, and how do you bind one to a URL?","a":"<p>A handler is a function that receives request data (as typed parameters) and returns a response. A decorator ties it to an HTTP verb and path, for example <code>@app.get('/health')</code>. FastAPI reads the signature to find path, query and body parameters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0023","card":"q-fastapi-005-3-handlers-running-the-server-sync-vs-as","sec":"fastapi-s006","q":"Which decorators does FastAPI provide for the different HTTP verbs?","a":"<p>One per verb on the app or router: <code>@app.get</code>, <code>@app.post</code>, <code>@app.put</code>, <code>@app.patch</code>, <code>@app.delete</code>, plus <code>head</code>, <code>options</code>, <code>trace</code>. Use the one matching the semantics of the operation, since clients, caches and docs rely on the verb.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0024","card":"q-fastapi-006-running","sec":"fastapi-s007","q":"What is the difference between <code>fastapi dev</code> and <code>fastapi run</code>?","a":"<p><code>fastapi dev server.py</code> is for development: it reloads when files change. <code>fastapi run server.py</code> is for production: no auto-reload. In recent <code>fastapi-cli</code> versions, <code>dev</code> listens on <code>127.0.0.1</code> only and <code>run</code> listens on <code>0.0.0.0</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0025","card":"q-fastapi-006-running","sec":"fastapi-s007","q":"Why is auto-reload a bad idea in production?","a":"<p>The reloader watches the file system and restarts the process on changes. That wastes CPU and memory, can restart the server in the middle of traffic, and is unnecessary because deployments replace the whole process or container anyway.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0026","card":"q-fastapi-006-running","sec":"fastapi-s007","q":"In a Python script, how do you start a FastAPI app with Uvicorn directly, and why guard it with <code>if __name__ == '__main__':</code>?","a":"<p>Call <code>uvicorn.run(app, port=8080)</code> inside the guard.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if __name__ == &#x27;__main__&#x27;:\n    import uvicorn\n    uvicorn.run(app, port=8080)</code></pre></div>\n<p>The guard makes the server start only when you run <code>python server.py</code>. When the <code>fastapi</code> CLI or another tool imports the module to get <code>app</code>, the server is not started a second time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0027","card":"q-fastapi-006-running","sec":"fastapi-s007","q":"How would you check from the command line that a FastAPI server is up?","a":"<p>Request a health endpoint with <code>curl http://localhost:8000/health</code>. FastAPI/Uvicorn listen on port 8000 by default. Add <code>-i</code> to see the status line and headers. Orchestrators such as Kubernetes use the same kind of endpoint for liveness and readiness probes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0028","card":"q-fastapi-007-sync-vs-async-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s008","q":"Why does FastAPI use asyncio, and what kind of workload benefits?","a":"<p>asyncio runs many tasks on one thread, switching when a task waits on I/O (network, database, files). It benefits I/O-bound services with many concurrent requests. It does not speed up CPU-bound code, because only one task runs at a time on the event loop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0029","card":"q-fastapi-007-sync-vs-async-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s008","q":"How does FastAPI run a handler declared with plain <code>def</code> versus <code>async def</code>?","a":"<p>A plain <code>def</code> handler runs in a thread pool, so it can block without stopping other requests. An <code>async def</code> handler runs directly on the event loop thread, so it must never block.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0030","card":"q-fastapi-007-sync-vs-async-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s008","q":"In FastAPI, what happens when you call <code>time.sleep(1)</code> inside an <code>async def</code> handler, and what should you do instead?","a":"<p><code>time.sleep</code> blocks the single event-loop thread, so every other request waits. With 10 concurrent requests the total time is about 10 s instead of 1 s. Use <code>await asyncio.sleep(1)</code>, or make the handler a plain <code>def</code> so it runs in the thread pool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0031","card":"q-fastapi-007-sync-vs-async-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s008","q":"You have 10 concurrent requests to a handler that waits 1 second. How long does each of these take in total: <code>def</code> with <code>time.sleep</code>, <code>async def</code> with <code>time.sleep</code>, <code>async def</code> with <code>asyncio.sleep</code>?","a":"<p><code>def</code> with <code>time.sleep</code>: about 1 s, since threads run in parallel (up to the thread pool limit). <code>async def</code> with <code>time.sleep</code>: about 10 s, since the event loop is blocked and the requests serialize. <code>async def</code> with <code>await asyncio.sleep</code>: about 1 s, since waiting is non-blocking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0032","card":"q-fastapi-007-sync-vs-async-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s008","q":"Should a new FastAPI project start with <code>def</code> or <code>async def</code> handlers?","a":"<p>Start with plain <code>def</code> handlers. They are safe even if they call blocking libraries. Move to <code>async def</code> when you measure a need, such as very many concurrent connections, and only if every library in the call chain is async or you offload blocking work to a thread.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0033","card":"q-fastapi-007-sync-vs-async-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s008","q":"Why is the sync-vs-async mistake hard to spot in a large code base?","a":"<p>A blocking call can hide deep inside a third-party library, such as a synchronous database driver, HTTP client or file read. The handler looks async, but one blocking call stalls the whole loop. Symptoms are latency that grows with concurrency while CPU stays low.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0034","card":"q-fastapi-007-sync-vs-async-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s008","q":"How can you call blocking code from inside an <code>async def</code> handler without freezing the event loop?","a":"<p>Run it in a worker thread: <code>await asyncio.to_thread(blocking_fn, arg)</code> or Starlette's <code>run_in_threadpool</code>. For CPU-heavy work, use a process pool or a task queue, because threads still share the GIL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0035","card":"q-fastapi-007-sync-vs-async-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s008","q":"A FastAPI service is slow under load, but CPU use is low and the handlers are <code>async def</code>. What do you check first?","a":"<p>Look for blocking calls inside async handlers: <code>time.sleep</code>, <code>requests.get</code>, a sync DB driver, synchronous file or CPU-heavy work. Fix by switching to async libraries (<code>httpx.AsyncClient</code>, async drivers), moving the call to a thread, or changing the handler to <code>def</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0036","card":"q-fastapi-008-example-info-handler","sec":"fastapi-s009","q":"In FastAPI, a handler returns <code>{'time': datetime.now(tz=UTC)}</code>. What does the client see and who converted it?","a":"<p>The client sees an ISO 8601 string such as <code>\"2024-01-02T13:42:09.123456Z\"</code>. FastAPI's JSON encoder converts the <code>datetime</code> automatically, so the handler does not need <code>isoformat()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0037","card":"q-fastapi-008-example-info-handler","sec":"fastapi-s009","q":"Why use <code>datetime.now(tz=UTC)</code> instead of <code>datetime.now()</code> or <code>datetime.utcnow()</code> in an API?","a":"<p><code>datetime.now()</code> returns a naive time in the server's local zone, which is ambiguous to clients. <code>datetime.utcnow()</code> returns a naive datetime that looks like UTC and is deprecated since Python 3.12. <code>datetime.now(tz=UTC)</code> is timezone-aware, so the serialized value carries its offset.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0038","card":"q-fastapi-009-query-parameters","sec":"fastapi-s010","q":"In FastAPI, how does a function parameter become a query parameter, and how does it differ from a path parameter?","a":"<p>A parameter that is not part of the route path and is not a Pydantic model becomes a query parameter, read from <code>?name=value</code>. A name inside braces in the route (<code>/users/{login}</code>) is a path parameter. Use the path for identifying a resource and query parameters for filtering, paging and options.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0039","card":"q-fastapi-009-query-parameters","sec":"fastapi-s010","q":"How do you make a query parameter required versus optional in FastAPI?","a":"<p>No default value means required. A default makes it optional, and the type hint can show it: <code>level: str | None = None</code>. A missing required parameter returns <code>422</code> automatically.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get(&#x27;/logs&#x27;)\ndef logs(start: str, level: str | None = None):\n    ...</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0040","card":"q-fastapi-009-query-parameters","sec":"fastapi-s010","q":"What happens in FastAPI if a client calls an endpoint with <code>start=banana</code> when the parameter is typed <code>start: datetime</code>?","a":"<p>FastAPI returns <code>422 Unprocessable Entity</code> with a JSON body listing the failing field, its location (<code>query</code>) and the reason. Your handler never runs. The type hint drives both parsing and validation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0041","card":"q-fastapi-009-query-parameters","sec":"fastapi-s010","q":"Why does the type hint on a query parameter matter in FastAPI?","a":"<p>It drives conversion (the string <code>\"5\"</code> becomes <code>int</code>, an ISO string becomes <code>datetime</code>), validation (bad values give <code>422</code>) and the OpenAPI schema shown in <code>/docs</code>. Without a hint the value stays a plain string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0042","card":"q-fastapi-009-query-parameters","sec":"fastapi-s010","q":"Type hints and <code>422</code> already validate parameters, so why validate them again in the handler, as in <code>if start &gt;= end</code>?","a":"<p>Type validation checks only the type and format. Rules between fields or business rules (start before end, known log level) need your code. Raise <code>HTTPException(status_code=400, detail=...)</code> so the client gets a clear error instead of a wrong result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0043","card":"q-fastapi-009-query-parameters","sec":"fastapi-s010","q":"How do you return an HTTP error with a chosen status code from a FastAPI handler?","a":"<p>Raise <code>HTTPException</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from fastapi import HTTPException\nraise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail=&#x27;no logs found&#x27;)</code></pre></div>\n<p>FastAPI turns it into a JSON response <code>{\"detail\": \"no logs found\"}</code> with that status. Use <code>http.HTTPStatus</code> constants for readable code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0044","card":"q-fastapi-009-query-parameters","sec":"fastapi-s010","q":"What should an endpoint return when a query is valid but matches nothing: <code>404</code> or <code>200</code> with an empty list?","a":"<p>It depends on the resource. For a lookup of a single item by id, <code>404</code> is right. For a search or list over a collection, <code>200</code> with an empty list is the usual choice, since the collection exists and is simply empty. Pick one rule and document it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0045","card":"q-fastapi-009-query-parameters","sec":"fastapi-s010","q":"How do you accept a list of values in one query parameter, such as <code>?tag=a&amp;tag=b</code>?","a":"<p>Declare the parameter as a list and mark it with <code>Query</code>, since a bare list would be read as a body.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI, Query\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get(&#x27;/items&#x27;)\ndef items(tag: list[str] = Query(default=[])):\n    return {&#x27;tags&#x27;: tag}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0046","card":"q-fastapi-010-request-body-pydantic-models","sec":"fastapi-s011","q":"In FastAPI, how does a handler receive and validate a JSON request body?","a":"<p>Declare a parameter whose type is a Pydantic <code>BaseModel</code>. FastAPI reads the JSON body, validates it against the model and passes you the instance. Invalid input never reaches your code and returns <code>422</code> with details.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0047","card":"q-fastapi-010-request-body-pydantic-models","sec":"fastapi-s011","q":"How do you constrain fields in a Pydantic model, such as a positive amount or a string of at least 2 characters?","a":"<p>Use <code>Field</code> with constraints.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass Sale(BaseModel):\n    sku: str = Field(min_length=2)\n    amount: int = Field(gt=0)</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>gt</code> is greater than, <code>ge</code> greater or equal, <code>lt</code> less than, <code>le</code> less or equal. Violations return <code>422</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0048","card":"q-fastapi-010-request-body-pydantic-models","sec":"fastapi-s011","q":"In Pydantic, how do you express that an integer must be between 1 and 64 inclusive?","a":"<p><code>Field(ge=1, le=64)</code>. Using <code>gt=0, lt=65</code> gives the same range for integers, but <code>ge</code>/<code>le</code> states the boundaries directly and avoids off-by-one mistakes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0049","card":"q-fastapi-010-request-body-pydantic-models","sec":"fastapi-s011","q":"Why keep API models (Pydantic) separate from database models?","a":"<p>The API contract and the storage format change for different reasons and may differ: dollars in the API versus integer cents in the database, hidden internal columns, different field names. Separate models let you evolve one without breaking the other and prevent leaking internal fields to clients.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0050","card":"q-fastapi-010-request-body-pydantic-models","sec":"fastapi-s011","q":"Why store money as integer cents in the database instead of a float?","a":"<p>Binary floats cannot represent most decimal fractions exactly, so sums drift (<code>0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3</code>). Integer cents are exact. Convert at the API edge, or use <code>Decimal</code> for exact values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0051","card":"q-fastapi-010-request-body-pydantic-models","sec":"fastapi-s011","q":"What does adding a return annotation like <code>-&gt; Sale</code> do to a FastAPI handler?","a":"<p>It adds the response schema to the OpenAPI docs and makes FastAPI validate and filter the returned data against that model. Fields not in the model are not sent, which helps avoid leaking internal fields. <code>response_model=</code> on the decorator achieves the same and lets the return type differ from the model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0052","card":"q-fastapi-010-request-body-pydantic-models","sec":"fastapi-s011","q":"How does FastAPI decide where each handler parameter comes from?","a":"<p>By rules: a name in the route path is a path parameter, a Pydantic model is the JSON body, and a simple type (<code>int</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>datetime</code>) not in the path is a query parameter. You can override this with <code>Query()</code>, <code>Header()</code>, <code>Cookie()</code>, <code>Body()</code> and <code>Form()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0053","card":"q-fastapi-010-request-body-pydantic-models","sec":"fastapi-s011","q":"What is the Pydantic v1 versus v2 difference for turning a model into a dict or JSON?","a":"<p>In Pydantic v2 use <code>model.model_dump()</code> and <code>model.model_dump_json()</code>. In v1 these were <code>.dict()</code> and <code>.json()</code>, which still work in v2 but are deprecated. FastAPI 0.100+ supports Pydantic v2.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0054","card":"q-fastapi-010-request-body-pydantic-models","sec":"fastapi-s011","q":"What do specialized Pydantic types such as <code>PastDatetime</code> or <code>EmailStr</code> give you over plain <code>datetime</code> or <code>str</code>?","a":"<p>Built-in validation for common rules without hand-written checks: <code>PastDatetime</code> rejects future datetimes, <code>EmailStr</code> checks email format (it needs the <code>email-validator</code> package). The rule is enforced at the API boundary and appears in the schema.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0055","card":"q-fastapi-011-html-forms","sec":"fastapi-s012","q":"How do you read HTML form fields in a FastAPI handler?","a":"<p>Declare the parameters with <code>Form()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from typing import Annotated\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Form\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.post(&#x27;/survey&#x27;)\ndef survey(name: Annotated[str, Form()]):\n    return {&#x27;name&#x27;: name}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The form field names in the HTML must match the parameter names. FastAPI needs the <code>python-multipart</code> package for this, which <code>fastapi[standard]</code> installs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0056","card":"q-fastapi-011-html-forms","sec":"fastapi-s012","q":"Why can't a FastAPI handler accept both a JSON body and form fields in the same endpoint?","a":"<p>A request has one body with one content type. Forms use <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> or <code>multipart/form-data</code>, JSON uses <code>application/json</code>. HTTP and FastAPI do not combine them, so choose one per endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0057","card":"q-fastapi-011-html-forms","sec":"fastapi-s012","q":"After handling an HTML form POST, why does a server usually answer with a redirect?","a":"<p>It follows the Post/Redirect/Get pattern: the browser then loads a normal GET page, so refreshing does not resubmit the form. In FastAPI use <code>RedirectResponse</code>. Prefer <code>303 See Other</code>, which forces the next request to be a GET.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0058","card":"q-fastapi-011-html-forms","sec":"fastapi-s012","q":"In FastAPI/Starlette, what status code does <code>RedirectResponse</code> use by default, and why does that matter after a form POST?","a":"<p>The default is <code>307 Temporary Redirect</code>, which tells the browser to repeat the same method and body. After a POST that would re-POST to the new URL. Pass <code>status_code=303</code> (or <code>302</code>) to get a GET to the target page.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0059","card":"q-fastapi-011-html-forms","sec":"fastapi-s012","q":"In FastAPI, how do you serve static HTML, CSS or JS files, and what does <code>app.mount</code> do?","a":"<p>Mount <code>StaticFiles</code> on a path: <code>app.mount('/static', StaticFiles(directory='static'))</code>. <code>mount</code> attaches an independent ASGI sub-application at that prefix, so requests under <code>/static</code> are served from the directory without going through your routes. In production, a CDN or reverse proxy usually serves static files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0060","card":"q-fastapi-011-html-forms","sec":"fastapi-s012","q":"Name a security issue with accepting HTML form input and logging it, as in <code>logging.info('name: %r', name)</code>.","a":"<p>Logging raw user input allows log injection (newlines forging fake log lines) and may store sensitive data. Using <code>%r</code> escapes newlines, which helps. Also avoid logging passwords or tokens, and limit the length of logged values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0061","card":"q-fastapi-012-raw-request-data","sec":"fastapi-s013","q":"When does a FastAPI handler need the <code>Request</code> object instead of typed parameters?","a":"<p>When the body is not JSON or form data (an uploaded image, CSV text, binary), or when you need raw headers, the client address or the body bytes. Typed parameters cover the common cases. <code>Request</code> gives full access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0062","card":"q-fastapi-012-raw-request-data","sec":"fastapi-s013","q":"In FastAPI, why must a handler that calls <code>await request.body()</code> be declared <code>async def</code>?","a":"<p><code>request.body()</code> is a coroutine, and <code>await</code> is only valid inside an <code>async def</code> function. The handler then runs on the event loop, so keep the work inside it non-blocking, or hand blocking calls such as image decoding to a thread.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0063","card":"q-fastapi-012-raw-request-data","sec":"fastapi-s013","q":"How do you read a request header in a FastAPI handler?","a":"<p>With the <code>Request</code> object: <code>request.headers.get('Content-Length')</code>. Header lookup is case-insensitive. Alternatively declare a parameter with <code>Header()</code>, which also validates the type and documents it in <code>/docs</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0064","card":"q-fastapi-012-raw-request-data","sec":"fastapi-s013","q":"Why should you limit the size of a request body in a public API, and how?","a":"<p>Without a limit an attacker can send gigabytes, exhausting memory or disk (denial of service). Limit it at the reverse proxy or server (for example nginx <code>client_max_body_size</code>) and also check in code. Reject with <code>413 Payload Too Large</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0065","card":"q-fastapi-012-raw-request-data","sec":"fastapi-s013","q":"Checking the <code>Content-Length</code> header before reading the body is a common size guard. What are its limits?","a":"<p>The header is client-supplied. It can be missing (chunked uploads) or false. The reliable approach is to enforce the limit while reading the body: iterate <code>request.stream()</code> and stop once the byte count exceeds the maximum. Also set a limit at the proxy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0066","card":"q-fastapi-012-raw-request-data","sec":"fastapi-s013","q":"What does <code>io.BytesIO</code> do when you pass request bytes to Pillow's <code>Image.open</code>?","a":"<p><code>BytesIO</code> wraps raw bytes in a file-like object in memory. Libraries such as Pillow expect a file or file-like object, so this avoids writing a temporary file to disk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0067","card":"q-fastapi-012-raw-request-data","sec":"fastapi-s013","q":"Raw bytes are decoded as an image. What should happen if the body is not a valid image?","a":"<p>Catch the decoding error and return <code>400</code> or <code>415</code> with a clear message. If the exception is not handled, FastAPI returns <code>500 Internal Server Error</code>, which wrongly signals a server fault for a bad client upload.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0068","card":"q-fastapi-013-example-start-stop-a-vm-enum-lock-path-p","sec":"fastapi-s014","q":"Why use an <code>Enum</code> field in a Pydantic model, such as an allowed set of image names?","a":"<p>It restricts the value to a fixed set. Any other value is rejected with <code>422</code>, and the allowed values show up in the OpenAPI schema. It is safer than checking strings by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0069","card":"q-fastapi-013-example-start-stop-a-vm-enum-lock-path-p","sec":"fastapi-s014","q":"In FastAPI, a model has an <code>Enum</code> field. Why do you call <code>.value</code> on it before writing it to a database?","a":"<p>The field holds an <code>Enum</code> member, not the raw string. A database driver cannot bind an <code>Enum</code> object. <code>.value</code> gives the underlying string or number.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0070","card":"q-fastapi-013-example-start-stop-a-vm-enum-lock-path-p","sec":"fastapi-s014","q":"Why is a <code>threading.Lock</code> used around an in-memory dict in FastAPI handlers declared with plain <code>def</code>?","a":"<p>Plain <code>def</code> handlers run in multiple threads at once. Compound operations on shared state, such as check-then-insert, can interleave and corrupt data. A lock makes the critical section run one thread at a time.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from threading import Lock\nlock = Lock()\nstate = {}\n\ndef put(key, value):\n    with lock:\n        state[key] = value</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0071","card":"q-fastapi-013-example-start-stop-a-vm-enum-lock-path-p","sec":"fastapi-s014","q":"Why is process-local state, such as a global dict guarded by a lock, a problem when deploying FastAPI with several workers?","a":"<p>Each worker is a separate process with its own memory, so the dict is not shared and a request can hit a worker that has no record of the item. A lock does not help across processes. Use an external store such as a database or Redis for shared state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0072","card":"q-fastapi-013-example-start-stop-a-vm-enum-lock-path-p","sec":"fastapi-s014","q":"In Python, a FastAPI stop endpoint keeps its items in an in-memory dict <code>vms</code>. Why use <code>vms.pop(id, None)</code> rather than <code>del vms[id]</code>?","a":"<p><code>dict.pop(key, default)</code> removes the key and returns its value, or returns <code>None</code> if missing. <code>del</code> raises <code>KeyError</code> when the key is absent. With <code>pop</code> you can check for <code>None</code> and return a clean <code>404</code> instead of a <code>500</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0073","card":"q-fastapi-013-example-start-stop-a-vm-enum-lock-path-p","sec":"fastapi-s014","q":"In FastAPI, how do you build an endpoint like <code>POST /vm/{id}/stop</code> that takes an id from the path?","a":"<p>Put the name in braces in the route and declare a function parameter with the same name and type.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.post(&#x27;/vm/{id}/stop&#x27;)\ndef stop_vm(id: str):\n    ...</code></pre></div>\n<p>A type hint such as <code>int</code> makes FastAPI validate and convert the value. An unknown id should return <code>404</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0074","card":"q-fastapi-013-example-start-stop-a-vm-enum-lock-path-p","sec":"fastapi-s014","q":"Why use <code>uuid4().hex</code> for identifiers handed out by an API instead of an incrementing counter?","a":"<p>UUIDv4 values are random and unguessable, so clients cannot enumerate other users' records and no central counter is needed. A counter is predictable, which is a broken-access-control risk if authorization is missing. UUIDs are not a substitute for authorization checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0075","card":"q-fastapi-013-example-start-stop-a-vm-enum-lock-path-p","sec":"fastapi-s014","q":"Why is <code>POST</code> suitable for a \"start\" or \"stop\" action endpoint and not <code>GET</code>?","a":"<p>These actions change state. <code>GET</code> must be safe and idempotent: browsers, caches and crawlers may call it freely. <code>POST</code> signals a state change. A more RESTful alternative is modeling the VM as a resource and updating its state with <code>PATCH</code> or deleting it with <code>DELETE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0076","card":"q-fastapi-014-json-responses-with-pydantic","sec":"fastapi-s015","q":"Why return a Pydantic model instead of a plain dict from a FastAPI handler?","a":"<p>The model documents the response schema in <code>/docs</code>, validates and serializes the output, and filters out fields you did not declare. A dict works but gives no schema and no checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0077","card":"q-fastapi-014-json-responses-with-pydantic","sec":"fastapi-s015","q":"In Pydantic v2, how is a <code>timedelta</code> field serialized to JSON by default, and how do you change it?","a":"<p>By default as an ISO 8601 duration string such as <code>\"PT1M27S\"</code>. (Pydantic v1 produced a number of seconds.) To change it in v2, add a field serializer.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from datetime import timedelta\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, field_serializer\n\nclass TimeResponse(BaseModel):\n    delta: timedelta\n\n    @field_serializer(&#x27;delta&#x27;)\n    def serialize_delta(self, v: timedelta) -&gt; int:\n        return int(v.total_seconds())</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0078","card":"q-fastapi-014-json-responses-with-pydantic","sec":"fastapi-s015","q":"In a Pydantic v2 <code>field_serializer</code>, why does the return annotation matter?","a":"<p>Pydantic uses it to build the serialization schema, so the OpenAPI docs and the output type agree. If the annotation says <code>int</code> while the function returns a string, clients and docs disagree on the contract.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0079","card":"q-fastapi-014-json-responses-with-pydantic","sec":"fastapi-s015","q":"How would you serialize a field differently in v1 versus v2 of Pydantic?","a":"<p>In v2 use <code>@field_serializer('field')</code> (or <code>@model_serializer</code> for the whole model). In v1 you used <code>json_encoders</code> in the model <code>Config</code>, which is deprecated in v2. FastAPI works with both, depending on the installed Pydantic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0080","card":"q-fastapi-014-json-responses-with-pydantic","sec":"fastapi-s015","q":"When a handler returns a Pydantic model, does FastAPI return the same object, or process it first?","a":"<p>FastAPI validates it against the response model, converts it to JSON-compatible data and then encodes it. If you return a <code>Response</code> object directly, this step is skipped.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0081","card":"q-fastapi-015-streaming-responses","sec":"fastapi-s016","q":"When should a FastAPI endpoint use <code>StreamingResponse</code>, and what is its main benefit?","a":"<p>When the data is large or produced incrementally: big exports, database cursors, logs, produced media. The server sends each item as it is produced, so memory stays flat and the client sees the first bytes early, instead of waiting for the full result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0082","card":"q-fastapi-015-streaming-responses","sec":"fastapi-s016","q":"Standard JSON cannot be streamed item by item. What format do you use instead, and why does it work?","a":"<p>JSON Lines (NDJSON): one complete JSON object per line, separated by newlines. A client can parse each line as soon as it arrives, while one big JSON array is invalid until the closing bracket. Set <code>media_type='application/x-ndjson'</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0083","card":"q-fastapi-015-streaming-responses","sec":"fastapi-s016","q":"In FastAPI, how do you stream results from a function that yields items?","a":"<p>Pass the generator to <code>StreamingResponse</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef lines():\n    for i in range(3):\n        yield f&#x27;{i}\\n&#x27;\n\n@app.get(&#x27;/stream&#x27;)\ndef stream():\n    return StreamingResponse(lines(), media_type=&#x27;text/plain&#x27;)</code></pre></div>\n<p>FastAPI iterates the generator and writes each yielded chunk. A sync generator runs in a thread pool, an async generator on the event loop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0084","card":"q-fastapi-015-streaming-responses","sec":"fastapi-s016","q":"What is chunked transfer encoding, and why does streaming use it?","a":"<p>When the total size is unknown, HTTP/1.1 sends the body in chunks, each prefixed by its size in hex, ending with a zero-length chunk. There is no <code>Content-Length</code> header. Streaming responses use it because the server does not know the final size when it starts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0085","card":"q-fastapi-015-streaming-responses","sec":"fastapi-s016","q":"How can you see the raw chunk framing of a streaming response with <code>curl</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>curl -i --raw URL</code>. <code>-i</code> prints response headers (you will see <code>Transfer-Encoding: chunked</code>) and <code>--raw</code> disables curl's own decoding of chunks, so you see the hex sizes between the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0086","card":"q-fastapi-015-streaming-responses","sec":"fastapi-s016","q":"What are the pitfalls of streaming a response from a database query?","a":"<p>Once the first bytes are sent, the status code <code>200</code> is already committed, so a later error cannot change it, and the client sees a truncated stream. Keep the DB connection open while streaming, and make sure clients can detect incomplete output (for example by a final marker line).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0087","card":"q-fastapi-015-streaming-responses","sec":"fastapi-s016","q":"What is the problem with an <code>async def</code> generator that does blocking work, passed to <code>StreamingResponse</code>?","a":"<p>An async generator runs on the event loop, so blocking calls in it freeze all requests, just as in an <code>async def</code> handler. Use a sync generator (run in a thread pool) or make the work non-blocking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0088","card":"q-fastapi-016-raw-non-json-responses","sec":"fastapi-s017","q":"How do you return an image, CSV or other non-JSON content from a FastAPI handler?","a":"<p>Return a <code>Response</code> with the content as bytes or str and a <code>media_type</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI, Response\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get(&#x27;/pixel&#x27;)\ndef pixel():\n    return Response(content=b&#x27;\\x89PNG...&#x27;, media_type=&#x27;image/png&#x27;)</code></pre></div>\n<p>The media type becomes the <code>Content-Type</code> header, so the client knows how to interpret the body.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0089","card":"q-fastapi-016-raw-non-json-responses","sec":"fastapi-s017","q":"What does <code>media_type</code> control in a FastAPI <code>Response</code>, and what goes wrong if you leave it out for an image?","a":"<p>It sets the <code>Content-Type</code> response header. Without it Starlette sends no content type for raw bytes. Browsers and clients may guess wrongly or download the file instead of displaying it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0090","card":"q-fastapi-016-raw-non-json-responses","sec":"fastapi-s017","q":"Why validate the inputs of an image resize endpoint (width, height, body size)?","a":"<p>Such parameters come from untrusted clients. A huge width and height can allocate gigabytes of memory, and a huge upload exhausts memory. Reject non-positive or excessive dimensions and oversized bodies with <code>400</code>/<code>413</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0091","card":"q-fastapi-016-raw-non-json-responses","sec":"fastapi-s017","q":"Can you document a raw <code>Response</code> in FastAPI's OpenAPI docs?","a":"<p>Yes. A raw <code>Response</code> bypasses response-model handling, so <code>/docs</code> shows no schema. Describe it with <code>responses={200: {'content': {'image/png': {}}}}</code> and <code>response_class=Response</code> on the decorator.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0092","card":"q-fastapi-016-raw-non-json-responses","sec":"fastapi-s017","q":"A handler returns <code>Response(content=..., status_code=HTTPStatus.OK, media_type='image/png')</code>. Which of FastAPI's conveniences do you lose compared with returning a dict?","a":"<p>Automatic JSON serialization, response-model validation and filtering, and the schema in the docs. You take over encoding and the content type yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0093","card":"q-fastapi-017-custom-error-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s018","q":"What happens in FastAPI when a handler raises an exception type FastAPI does not know?","a":"<p>The server returns <code>500 Internal Server Error</code> with a generic body, and the traceback is logged. For errors that really are client mistakes, that is the wrong status. Map them to proper responses with a custom exception handler.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0094","card":"q-fastapi-017-custom-error-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s018","q":"How do you register a handler that turns your own exception class into a <code>400</code> JSON response in FastAPI?","a":"<p>Use <code>@app.exception_handler(MyError)</code> on an <code>async def (request, exc)</code> function that returns a <code>JSONResponse</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI, Request\nfrom fastapi.responses import JSONResponse\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nclass MyError(Exception):\n    pass\n\n@app.exception_handler(MyError)\nasync def my_error_handler(request: Request, exc: MyError):\n    return JSONResponse(status_code=400, content={&#x27;error&#x27;: str(exc)})</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0095","card":"q-fastapi-017-custom-error-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s018","q":"Why is a central exception handler better than catching the error in every route?","a":"<p>One place defines the mapping from domain errors to HTTP responses, so status codes and error formats stay consistent. Business functions can raise plain exceptions without knowing about HTTP, and handlers stay short.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0096","card":"q-fastapi-017-custom-error-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s018","q":"What is the risk of returning <code>str(exc)</code> to clients from an error handler?","a":"<p>The message may contain internal details: file paths, SQL, tokens, hostnames or user data. This is information disclosure (OWASP). Return a safe, fixed message to the client and log the full exception on the server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0097","card":"q-fastapi-017-custom-error-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s018","q":"In FastAPI, what is the difference between raising <code>HTTPException</code> and registering your own exception handler?","a":"<p><code>HTTPException</code> is for one-off errors raised in a route, with a status and detail. A custom handler maps a whole exception type, possibly raised deep in your business logic, to a response with custom body and headers. Use the handler when many routes can raise the same error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0098","card":"q-fastapi-017-custom-error-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s018","q":"How can you customize the <code>422</code> response FastAPI sends on validation failure?","a":"<p>Register a handler for <code>RequestValidationError</code> (from <code>fastapi.exceptions</code>) with <code>@app.exception_handler(RequestValidationError)</code> and return your own <code>JSONResponse</code>. Do it to match a company-wide error format, but do not leak internal details.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0099","card":"q-fastapi-017-custom-error-handlers","sec":"fastapi-s018","q":"Why do custom response headers conventionally start with <code>X-</code>, and are header names case-sensitive?","a":"<p>The <code>X-</code> prefix marked non-standard headers. RFC 6648 deprecated this convention for new headers, but it is still widely used. Header names are case-insensitive in HTTP; header values may be case-sensitive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0100","card":"q-fastapi-018-example-query-logs-with-content-negotiat","sec":"fastapi-s019","q":"What is content negotiation in HTTP, and which header does the client use?","a":"<p>The client states which formats it can accept in the <code>Accept</code> header (for example <code>application/json</code> or <code>text/csv</code>), and the server picks one, setting <code>Content-Type</code> on the response. If it cannot serve any, it answers <code>406 Not Acceptable</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0101","card":"q-fastapi-018-example-query-logs-with-content-negotiat","sec":"fastapi-s019","q":"How should a server treat <code>Accept: */*</code> and a missing <code>Accept</code> header?","a":"<p>Both mean the client accepts anything, so the server may choose its default format, usually JSON. <code>curl</code> sends <code>*/*</code> by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0102","card":"q-fastapi-018-example-query-logs-with-content-negotiat","sec":"fastapi-s019","q":"Why is comparing the <code>Accept</code> header with <code>==</code> fragile in a content negotiation endpoint?","a":"<p>Real clients send lists with quality weights, such as <code>text/csv, application/json;q=0.9, */*;q=0.1</code>. An equality check fails on those. Parse the list, honor the <code>q</code> values and choose the best supported type, or use a library for it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0103","card":"q-fastapi-018-example-query-logs-with-content-negotiat","sec":"fastapi-s019","q":"Which status code fits a request whose <code>Accept</code> asks only for a type you cannot produce?","a":"<p><code>406 Not Acceptable</code>. A <code>400</code> is also used for malformed requests, but <code>406</code> describes this case precisely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0104","card":"q-fastapi-018-example-query-logs-with-content-negotiat","sec":"fastapi-s019","q":"How do you return CSV from FastAPI built from a list of dicts?","a":"<p>Write with <code>csv.DictWriter</code> into a <code>StringIO</code> and return a <code>Response</code> with <code>media_type='text/csv'</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import csv\nfrom io import StringIO\nfrom fastapi import Response\n\ndef to_csv(rows: list[dict]) -&gt; Response:\n    out = StringIO()\n    writer = csv.DictWriter(out, fieldnames=[&#x27;time&#x27;, &#x27;level&#x27;])\n    writer.writeheader()\n    writer.writerows(rows)\n    return Response(content=out.getvalue(), media_type=&#x27;text/csv&#x27;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0105","card":"q-fastapi-018-example-query-logs-with-content-negotiat","sec":"fastapi-s019","q":"Why call <code>datetime.isoformat()</code> when writing datetimes into a CSV in a FastAPI endpoint?","a":"<p>The CSV writer uses <code>str()</code>, which gives a space-separated format that is not ISO 8601. <code>isoformat()</code> gives an unambiguous, standard format that other tools parse. In JSON responses FastAPI does this conversion for you, but a manual CSV does not.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0106","card":"q-fastapi-018-example-query-logs-with-content-negotiat","sec":"fastapi-s019","q":"What are the paging parameters <code>count</code> and <code>offset</code> for, and how should an endpoint validate them?","a":"<p>They implement paging: <code>offset</code> is how many records to skip and <code>count</code> the page size. Reject negative offsets and non-positive counts, and cap the maximum count so a client cannot request the whole table in one call. For large or changing data, cursor (keyset) paging is more stable than offsets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0107","card":"q-fastapi-018-example-query-logs-with-content-negotiat","sec":"fastapi-s019","q":"Why might an endpoint return different response types, such as a Pydantic model for JSON and a <code>Response</code> for CSV, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>JSON benefits from model validation and docs, while CSV needs a raw <code>Response</code>. The trade-off is that the docs show only the JSON schema unless you declare the extra <code>responses</code>. The alternative is separate endpoints per format, which is simpler to document and cache.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0108","card":"q-fastapi-019-configuration","sec":"fastapi-s020","q":"Why separate configuration from code in a service?","a":"<p>The same build must run in development, staging and production with different ports, log levels, endpoints and secrets. Config outside the code lets you change behavior without a code change or rebuild, and keeps secrets out of version control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0109","card":"q-fastapi-019-configuration","sec":"fastapi-s020","q":"What is the usual precedence order for configuration, from lowest to highest?","a":"<p>Defaults in code, then a config file, then environment variables, then command-line arguments. Each layer overrides the previous one, so the most specific and most recent source wins. Containers commonly use environment variables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0110","card":"q-fastapi-019-configuration","sec":"fastapi-s020","q":"In Dynaconf, what does <code>envvar_prefix='SERVER'</code> do?","a":"<p>Environment variables starting with <code>SERVER_</code> override file settings, so <code>SERVER_PORT=9999</code> sets <code>settings.port</code> to 9999. The prefix avoids clashes with unrelated environment variables.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from dynaconf import Dynaconf\n\nsettings = Dynaconf(\n    envvar_prefix=&#x27;SERVER&#x27;,\n    settings_files=[&#x27;settings.yaml&#x27;, &#x27;.secrets.yaml&#x27;],\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0111","card":"q-fastapi-019-configuration","sec":"fastapi-s020","q":"Where should secrets such as API tokens live, and why a separate <code>.secrets.yaml</code> file?","a":"<p>Never in source code or the main config. A separate secrets file (kept out of Git through <code>.gitignore</code>) or a secret manager keeps them apart from non-sensitive settings, so config can be committed and reviewed. In cloud deployments prefer a managed secret store injected as environment variables or mounted files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0112","card":"q-fastapi-019-configuration","sec":"fastapi-s020","q":"Why validate configuration at startup, as in checking that a port is between 0 and 65535?","a":"<p>Bad config is a frequent cause of outages. Failing fast at startup with a clear message is better than starting with a broken setting and failing at runtime. A validation library such as Pydantic Settings can do this declaratively.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0113","card":"q-fastapi-019-configuration","sec":"fastapi-s020","q":"When using <code>argparse</code> to override a setting, why use <code>default=settings.port</code> for the argument?","a":"<p>The command-line argument then falls back to the value from file or environment when no flag is passed. Only an explicit flag overrides it, which gives the layering: file, then env, then CLI.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from argparse import ArgumentParser\n\nparser = ArgumentParser()\n# settings is the config object already loaded from file and environment\nparser.add_argument(&#x27;--port&#x27;, type=int, default=settings.port)\nargs = parser.parse_args()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0114","card":"q-fastapi-019-configuration","sec":"fastapi-s020","q":"In FastAPI, what is the alternative to Dynaconf for typed settings?","a":"<p><code>pydantic-settings</code> (<code>BaseSettings</code>). It reads environment variables and <code>.env</code> files into a typed, validated class. FastAPI's docs recommend it, and it plugs into <code>Depends</code> for injection and test overrides. In Pydantic v2 <code>BaseSettings</code> moved out of <code>pydantic</code> into the separate <code>pydantic-settings</code> package.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0115","card":"q-fastapi-020-logging-and-metrics","sec":"fastapi-s021","q":"What is the difference between logs and metrics, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Metrics are numeric time series (request count, error rate, latency) that answer \"what is happening now?\" and drive dashboards and alerts. Logs are event records that answer \"how did we get here?\" and are used for debugging and incident analysis. Use metrics to detect problems and logs to diagnose them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0116","card":"q-fastapi-020-logging-and-metrics","sec":"fastapi-s021","q":"Why use Python's <code>logging</code> module instead of <code>print</code> in a server?","a":"<p><code>logging</code> has levels, timestamps, formats, handlers (files, stdout, remote) and per-module loggers, and it can be configured without code changes. <code>print</code> has none of these and is hard to filter or route.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0117","card":"q-fastapi-020-logging-and-metrics","sec":"fastapi-s021","q":"How do you configure basic logging in Python so each line has a timestamp and level?","a":"<p>Call <code>logging.basicConfig</code> early, once, before other code logs.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(\n    level=logging.INFO,\n    format=&#x27;%(asctime)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s&#x27;,\n    datefmt=&#x27;%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S&#x27;,\n)</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>basicConfig</code> does nothing if the root logger already has handlers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0118","card":"q-fastapi-020-logging-and-metrics","sec":"fastapi-s021","q":"Why write <code>logging.info('get posts for %s', login)</code> rather than an f-string?","a":"<p>The arguments are formatted lazily, only if the record is actually emitted at that level, so disabled debug logs cost almost nothing. It also keeps the message template constant, which helps grouping, and avoids formatting errors breaking the call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0119","card":"q-fastapi-020-logging-and-metrics","sec":"fastapi-s021","q":"What should you log in a handler, and at what level?","a":"<p>Log enough to reproduce a problem: key inputs such as query parameters, decisions (rejected input), and errors with context. Use <code>INFO</code> for normal events, <code>WARNING</code> for suspicious but handled cases, <code>ERROR</code> for failures, <code>DEBUG</code> for detail. Never log secrets or personal data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0120","card":"q-fastapi-020-logging-and-metrics","sec":"fastapi-s021","q":"In FastAPI, how do you measure the duration of every request in one place?","a":"<p>Use HTTP middleware. <code>call_next</code> runs the rest of the chain and returns the response, so you can time around it.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from time import perf_counter\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Request\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.middleware(&#x27;http&#x27;)\nasync def timing(request: Request, call_next):\n    start = perf_counter()\n    response = await call_next(request)\n    duration = perf_counter() - start\n    response.headers[&#x27;X-Process-Time&#x27;] = f&#x27;{duration:.3f}&#x27;\n    return response</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0121","card":"q-fastapi-020-logging-and-metrics","sec":"fastapi-s021","q":"Why use <code>time.perf_counter()</code> and not <code>time.time()</code> for measuring durations?","a":"<p><code>perf_counter</code> is a monotonic, high-resolution clock. <code>time.time()</code> follows the system clock, which can jump (NTP adjustments, manual changes), giving negative or wrong durations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0122","card":"q-fastapi-020-logging-and-metrics","sec":"fastapi-s021","q":"What does <code>call_next</code> do in a FastAPI <code>@app.middleware('http')</code> function, and what happens if you never call it?","a":"<p>It passes the request on to the next middleware or the route handler and returns the response. If you never call it, the request never reaches the handler, which is how you short-circuit a request, for example to reject it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0123","card":"q-fastapi-020-logging-and-metrics","sec":"fastapi-s021","q":"What is a convention for emitting metrics through plain log lines, and what is the trade-off versus a metrics library?","a":"<p>Prefix lines with a fixed marker such as <code>[metric:call.duration]</code> so a log collector can parse them and forward numbers to a metrics system. It needs no new dependency. The downside is parsing cost and weaker semantics. A library such as Prometheus client or OpenTelemetry gives counters, histograms and labels directly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0124","card":"q-fastapi-020-logging-and-metrics","sec":"fastapi-s021","q":"How can you log through Uvicorn's logger, and why might you want to?","a":"<p><code>logging.getLogger('uvicorn')</code>. It shares Uvicorn's handlers and format, so app messages appear alongside server access logs. Separate named loggers for the server and the app let you set levels independently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0125","card":"q-fastapi-021-security","sec":"fastapi-s022","q":"What is the OWASP Top 10, and why should a backend developer know it?","a":"<p>It is a regularly updated list of the most critical web application security risks, such as broken access control, cryptographic failures and injection. It gives a shared checklist for design and code review.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0126","card":"q-fastapi-021-security","sec":"fastapi-s022","q":"What does \"put on a security hat\" mean when reviewing an endpoint?","a":"<p>Review the code as an attacker: who can call this, what input can I send, what happens with huge, malformed or malicious values, and what can I read or overwrite that I should not? Developers normally think about how the code should work, so this deliberately flips the view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0127","card":"q-fastapi-021-security","sec":"fastapi-s022","q":"A FastAPI service has <code>POST /users/{login}</code> that creates or overwrites users with no authentication and takes <code>is_admin</code> from the body. What is wrong?","a":"<p>Broken access control: anyone can create or overwrite users, including making themselves admin. Fix it by requiring authentication and checking authorization (only admins may create users or set <code>is_admin</code>), never trusting privilege fields from the client, and rejecting overwrites of existing users unless intended.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0128","card":"q-fastapi-021-security","sec":"fastapi-s022","q":"Why validate a path parameter like <code>login</code> even though it is a <code>str</code>?","a":"<p>The type only says it is text. Without constraints it can contain path separators, SQL, control characters or huge strings. Constrain it with <code>Path(pattern=...)</code> (FastAPI 0.100+ with Pydantic v2; older versions used <code>regex=</code>) or <code>min_length</code>/<code>max_length</code>, and still use parameterized queries and safe handling downstream.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from typing import Annotated\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Path\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get(&#x27;/users/{login}&#x27;)\ndef get_user(login: Annotated[str, Path(pattern=r&#x27;^[a-z0-9_]{3,32}$&#x27;)]):\n    ...</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0129","card":"q-fastapi-021-security","sec":"fastapi-s022","q":"How do you protect FastAPI endpoints with authentication?","a":"<p>Use a security dependency such as <code>OAuth2PasswordBearer</code> or <code>HTTPBearer</code>, or an API key header dependency, which extracts and validates a token and returns the current user. Add it to routes with <code>Depends</code>, or to a whole router. Verify tokens such as JWTs properly (signature, expiry, audience) and use HTTPS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0130","card":"q-fastapi-021-security","sec":"fastapi-s022","q":"What is the difference between authentication and authorization, with a FastAPI example?","a":"<p>Authentication proves who the caller is (a valid token gives the user). Authorization decides what that user may do (is this user an admin, or the owner of this record). In FastAPI both are commonly dependencies: <code>get_current_user</code> authenticates, <code>require_admin</code> authorizes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0131","card":"q-fastapi-021-security","sec":"fastapi-s022","q":"Why does returning <code>f'{login!r} not found'</code> for a missing user help attackers, and what is a better design?","a":"<p>It lets attackers enumerate which accounts exist, and echoing input can enable injection in clients. Return generic messages where enumeration matters (for example login), and keep detailed errors in the logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0132","card":"q-fastapi-022-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"Why can't you open a database connection in <code>if __name__ == '__main__':</code> when running a FastAPI app with the <code>fastapi</code> CLI?","a":"<p>The CLI imports the module and runs the app itself, so the <code>__main__</code> block never executes. FastAPI's answer is the lifespan hook for startup and shutdown, plus dependencies for per-request objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0133","card":"q-fastapi-022-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"What is the FastAPI lifespan function, and how is it structured?","a":"<p>An async context manager passed as <code>FastAPI(lifespan=...)</code>. Code before <code>yield</code> runs at startup, code after it at shutdown. Put the close in <code>finally</code> so it runs even after errors.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app: FastAPI):\n    # startup: open connections, load models\n    try:\n        yield\n    finally:\n        pass  # shutdown: close connections\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0134","card":"q-fastapi-022-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"How did startup and shutdown work in older FastAPI versions, and what is recommended now?","a":"<p>Older code used <code>@app.on_event('startup')</code> and <code>@app.on_event('shutdown')</code>. These are deprecated in favor of the <code>lifespan</code> parameter, which keeps setup and teardown together and can share state between them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0135","card":"q-fastapi-022-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"Why use <code>try/finally</code> around <code>yield</code> in a lifespan function?","a":"<p>If the app stops because of an error, an unprotected cleanup after <code>yield</code> may not run. <code>finally</code> guarantees the connection is closed, files are released and buffers flushed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0136","card":"q-fastapi-022-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"What does <code>check_same_thread=False</code> do when connecting to SQLite from FastAPI, and what is the risk?","a":"<p>By default <code>sqlite3</code> refuses to use a connection from a thread other than the one that created it. FastAPI runs <code>def</code> handlers in a thread pool, so a shared connection needs <code>check_same_thread=False</code>. The risk is concurrent use of one connection. Serialize access with a lock, or use one connection per request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0137","card":"q-fastapi-022-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"Why does each request need its own database cursor or session instead of one shared object?","a":"<p>Cursors and sessions hold state (current query, transaction). Requests run concurrently, so sharing them mixes results and transactions. A per-request object isolates work and lets you commit or roll back per request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0138","card":"q-fastapi-022-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"In FastAPI, what is dependency injection, and what problem does <code>Depends</code> solve?","a":"<p>You declare what a handler needs as parameters with <code>Depends(fn)</code>, and FastAPI calls <code>fn</code> for each request and passes the result in. It avoids globals and repeated setup code, keeps handlers focused, and lets tests replace dependencies through <code>app.dependency_overrides</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0139","card":"q-fastapi-022-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"How does a dependency that uses <code>yield</code> work in FastAPI, and what is the benefit?","a":"<p>Code before <code>yield</code> sets up a resource, the yielded value is injected into the handler, and code after <code>yield</code> is cleanup that runs when the request is done. It suits per-request objects such as DB sessions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from fastapi import Depends, FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef get_session():\n    session = open_session()  # placeholder for your session factory\n    try:\n        yield session\n    finally:\n        session.close()\n\n@app.get(&#x27;/items&#x27;)\ndef items(session=Depends(get_session)):\n    ...</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0140","card":"q-fastapi-023-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"What does using a <code>sqlite3</code> connection in a <code>with</code> block do?","a":"<p>It manages the transaction: the transaction is committed if the block ends normally and rolled back if an exception is raised. It does not close the connection, so you still need to call <code>close()</code> yourself, for example during shutdown.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0141","card":"q-fastapi-023-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"Why use named, parameterized queries like <code>INSERT ... VALUES (:user, :time)</code> instead of building SQL with f-strings?","a":"<p>The driver sends values separately from the SQL text, so input cannot change the query structure. That prevents SQL injection, which is also a top OWASP category. It also handles quoting and types correctly, and allows statement reuse.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cursor.execute(\n    &#x27;INSERT INTO trades (user, side) VALUES (:user, :side)&#x27;,\n    {&#x27;user&#x27;: user, &#x27;side&#x27;: side},\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0142","card":"q-fastapi-023-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"In a FastAPI handler, how do you receive a request body and a database cursor at the same time?","a":"<p>Declare the body as a Pydantic model parameter and the cursor as a <code>Depends</code> parameter. FastAPI reads the body from the request, calls the dependency, and passes both in.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@app.post(&#x27;/trades&#x27;)\ndef new_trade(trade: Trade, cursor=Depends(get_cursor)):\n    ...</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0143","card":"q-fastapi-023-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"In Pydantic, why not trust <code>Field(min_length=3)</code>-style validation as the only protection for data written to a database?","a":"<p>It validates shape and ranges but does not stop SQL injection or enforce uniqueness and foreign keys. Use parameterized queries and database constraints (NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK) as the last line of defense.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0144","card":"q-fastapi-023-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"For a production FastAPI service, why consider a connection pool or an ORM session instead of a single shared SQLite connection?","a":"<p>A single connection serializes access and is not designed for concurrent writers. A pool, such as SQLAlchemy's, hands out a connection per request, handles reconnects and limits concurrency to what the database can bear. SQLite is fine for small apps and tests, while client-server databases such as PostgreSQL suit concurrent services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0145","card":"q-fastapi-023-databases-lifespan-and-dependency-inject","sec":"fastapi-s023","q":"How do you test a FastAPI handler that depends on a database without touching the real one?","a":"<p>Override the dependency in tests: set <code>app.dependency_overrides[get_cursor] = fake_get_cursor</code> and use <code>TestClient</code> (which needs <code>httpx</code>). Clear the overrides afterwards. This is a main benefit of dependency injection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0146","card":"q-fastapi-024-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"A FastAPI endpoint accepts a CSV upload and reads <code>await request.body()</code> with no checks. Which production problems does it have?","a":"<p>No content-type check, malformed CSV or empty files that crash the parser (a <code>500</code> instead of <code>400</code>), no logging or timing metrics, and a risk of long CPU-bound parsing on the event loop. Fix by validating size and type, handling parse errors, adding logs and metrics, and moving heavy parsing off the loop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0147","card":"q-fastapi-024-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"Which status code should an endpoint return when the request body type is not allowed, <code>406</code> or <code>415</code>?","a":"<p><code>415 Unsupported Media Type</code> is the precise code for an unsupported request <code>Content-Type</code>. <code>406 Not Acceptable</code> relates to the response format the client asked for in <code>Accept</code>. Mixing them up is a common mistake.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0148","card":"q-fastapi-024-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"In a handler, <code>request.headers['content-type']</code> is used to check the content type. What is the pitfall, and what is better?","a":"<p>Indexing raises <code>KeyError</code> if the header is missing, which turns into a <code>500</code>. Use <code>request.headers.get('content-type')</code> and treat missing or wrong values as <code>415</code>. Also remember the header may include parameters such as <code>text/csv; charset=utf-8</code>, so compare only the media type part.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0149","card":"q-fastapi-024-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"Why decode the CSV in a handler with a fixed <code>data.decode()</code> and what can go wrong?","a":"<p><code>bytes.decode()</code> uses UTF-8 and raises <code>UnicodeDecodeError</code> on other encodings, which becomes a <code>500</code> if unhandled. Catch the error and return <code>400</code>, or accept a declared charset. Files from spreadsheet tools sometimes start with a byte-order mark, so <code>utf-8-sig</code> may be needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0150","card":"q-fastapi-024-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"Why compute an average speed as distance divided by duration without guarding the denominator?","a":"<p>A file with one row, or identical timestamps, gives a duration of zero (<code>ZeroDivisionError</code>) or leaves the start or end time unset. Validate input first and return <code>400</code> with a clear message instead of a <code>500</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0151","card":"q-fastapi-024-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"Why is parsing a large CSV inside an <code>async def</code> handler a concern?","a":"<p>Parsing is CPU-bound and synchronous, so it blocks the event loop while it runs and delays all other requests. Use a plain <code>def</code> handler, which runs in a thread pool, or offload to a worker. For very large files, stream the input and process it in chunks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0152","card":"q-fastapi-025-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"In Python, what does <code>functools.wraps</code> do in a timing decorator, and why does it matter for a FastAPI route?","a":"<p>It copies the wrapped function's name, docstring and signature information onto the wrapper. FastAPI inspects the handler's signature to find parameters. Without <code>wraps</code>, it would see <code>*args, **kw</code> and could not bind the request or parameters correctly, and <code>/docs</code> would show the wrong name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0153","card":"q-fastapi-025-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"In FastAPI, in what order must <code>@app.post(...)</code> and your own decorator be applied, and why?","a":"<p><code>@app.post(...)</code> must be the outermost (top) decorator, with yours placed between it and the function. Decorators apply bottom up, and <code>app.post</code> registers whatever function it receives. If it sits underneath, it registers the unwrapped function and your wrapper is never used.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@app.post(&#x27;/run&#x27;)\n@timed  # your own decorator, applied first\nasync def run_stats(request: Request):\n    ...</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0154","card":"q-fastapi-025-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"Why put the duration logging in a <code>finally</code> block of a timing decorator?","a":"<p><code>finally</code> runs whether the function returns or raises. The duration is recorded for failed requests too, which are often the slow ones you most want to see.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import logging\nfrom time import perf_counter\n\nstart = perf_counter()\ntry:\n    result = work()\nfinally:\n    logging.info(&#x27;duration %.3f&#x27;, perf_counter() - start)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0155","card":"q-fastapi-025-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"When writing a timing decorator for an <code>async def</code> handler, why must the wrapper be <code>async</code> and <code>await</code> the function?","a":"<p>Calling an async function only creates a coroutine without running it. A sync wrapper would time the creation, not the work, and return an un-awaited coroutine. The wrapper must <code>await fn(...)</code> inside the timed section.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0156","card":"q-fastapi-025-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"Middleware versus a decorator for timing in FastAPI: how do they differ and when do you choose each?","a":"<p>Middleware wraps every HTTP request, so it is right for cross-cutting metrics such as total request time. A decorator wraps selected functions and can time inner steps, such as just the parsing. Dependencies are a third option when a check applies to a group of routes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0157","card":"q-fastapi-025-example-make-a-csv-processing-server-pro","sec":"fastapi-s024","q":"Which things should the logs of a production endpoint include to help during an incident?","a":"<p>Each decision and failure: rejected input (reason and size or type), the outcome with key figures, and the duration. Include a request id so lines of one request can be correlated. Exclude secrets and raw personal data, and use the right levels.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0158","card":"q-fastapi-026-7-quick-patterns-recap","sec":"fastapi-s025","q":"In FastAPI, which mechanism would you pick for startup objects, per-request objects, cross-cutting request timing and custom error responses?","a":"<p>Startup and shutdown objects: a <code>lifespan</code> function. Per-request objects: a dependency with <code>Depends</code> (using <code>yield</code> for cleanup). Cross-cutting timing or headers on every request: <code>@app.middleware('http')</code>. Custom error responses for a type of exception: <code>@app.exception_handler</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fastapi-f0159","card":"q-fastapi-026-7-quick-patterns-recap","sec":"fastapi-s025","q":"In FastAPI, how do you choose between a path parameter, a query parameter, a JSON body, a form and the raw <code>Request</code>?","a":"<p>Path parameter to identify a resource. Query parameters for filters, paging and options. A Pydantic JSON body for structured data being created or changed. <code>Form()</code> for browser form posts. The raw <code>Request</code> for non-JSON payloads such as images or CSV, where you must check size and type yourself.</p>","hand":true}];
